claire: Opened PR to expand Troubleshooting -> Rate limits. New bits: note about Retry-After formats (seconds vs HTTP-date per RFC7231), tokenizer-aware trimming example, and an explicit exponential-backoff-with-jitter snippet. Need quick review on wording and examples.

ben: Nice. One request: please call out the HTTP-date format for Retry-After because CDNs sometimes send that not seconds. A tiny parsing example will save support time.

claire: Good call. I added a small Python parse example showing try-int then parse HTTP-date fallback.

ben: Also add a short formula for jittered exponential backoff so teams don't implement naive fixed sleeps. Something like: sleep = min(max_ms, base_ms * 2**attempt) * (0.5 + rand()).

sophia: I'll tidy the JS snippet to use that jitter formula and include a max-attempts cap. Also adding an example that falls back to a smaller model after N attempts.

claire: Draft JS backoff snippet (in PR): ```js
// jittered exponential backoff (base in ms)
function sleepMs(ms){ return new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); }
async function retryWithBackoff(fn, baseMs=300, maxMs=8000, attempts=5){
  for(let i=0;i<attempts;i++){
    const res = await fn();
    if (!isRateLimit(res)) return res;
    const backoff = Math.min(maxMs, baseMs * 2**i);
    const jittered = Math.floor(backoff * (0.5 + Math.random()/1));
    await sleepMs(jittered);
  }
  // final fallback
  return await fn({ model: 'redwood-medium' });
}
```

sophia: Looks good. Minor: use 0.5 + Math.random()/2 for narrower jitter range. Also add detection helper: check resp.status===429 || resp.error?.code==='rate_limit'.

ben: For the tokenizer-aware trimming, recommend showing pseudo-code that uses the token count library rather than naive char count. That prevents regressing on multibyte texts.

claire: Added pseudo-code in PR: drop earliest tokens until budget fits, then append completion budget. Also added a one-line note: prefer prefix trimming to preserve recent turns.

